  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      46.8,
      409.41,
      237.35,
      429.34
    ],
    "text": "The Opportunity Ahead",
    "fonts": [
      "TimesNewRomanPS-BoldMT"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      18.0
    ]
  },
  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      46.8,
      436.87,
      568.06,
      528.77
    ],
    "text": "The Government of Sri Lanka, being the country\u2019s single largest buyer, digitalization of public procurement opens up a significant market opportunity for businesses across the country. It is envisaged that a diverse supplier base will continue to grow ensuring greater competition, better value for public money and a more inclusive e-GP ecosystem. Expanding the e-GP registered supplier base will be facilitated by the Ministry of Finance to increase competition, improving access to government procurement opportunities and maximizing the benefits of e-GP. Therefore, vendor registration fees on promise.lk will be waived for a limited period from September 2026 onwards to encourage potential suppliers to register free of charge.",
    "fonts": [
      "TimesNewRomanPSMT"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      11.039999961853027
    ]
  },
  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      46.8,
      534.79,
      568.06,
      586.85
    ],
    "text": "Despite Sri Lanka\u2019s late entry to the e-GP journey than many countries in the region, it creates an ideal opportunity for Sri Lanka to learn from other\u2019s experiences and rollout a more robust e-GP system from global experience and accelerate implementation. Promise.lk, as an established national platform with 324 active Procuring Entities is geared for significant transformation in the procurement landscape in the country.",
    "fonts": [
      "TimesNewRomanPSMT"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      11.039999961853027
    ]
  },
  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      46.8,
      593.02,
      568.04,
      631.76
    ],
    "text": "Currently, 90% of the Departments under the General Treasury undertake the routine procurement through e-GP platform. To promote adoption, the Treasury has made it mandatory the Goods procurements within the applicable limits in General Treasury Departments to be conducted through e-GP.",
    "fonts": [
      "TimesNewRomanPSMT"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      11.039999961853027
    ]
  },
  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      46.8,
      637.78,
      568.06,
      689.84
    ],
    "text": "The next phase of e-Government procurement system will be launched in 2027. Designed to handle both National and International Competitive Bidding, the platform will support procurement for goods, works, consulting services and non-consulting services. It is envisaged that advanced procurement features such as reverse auctions and e-catalogues will also be made available to use, once adaptation improves.",
    "fonts": [
      "TimesNewRomanPSMT"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      11.039999961853027
    ]
  }
]